Markowitz, an Applied AI

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.000
Engineer here at Google,
and Sara Robinson,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.625
a Developer Advocate
focused on AI and ML

00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:58.060
and I'm huge fans of you both.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.675
So, Dale, Sara,
how are you doing?

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:02.050
Thank you so much
for being here!

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.883
DALE MARKOWITZ: Thanks
for having us, Steph.

00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:06.027
I feel like I've been
seeing a lot of you lately.

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:07.360
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm omnipresent.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.100
SARA ROBINSON: Doing
good over here.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.840
STEPHANIE WONG: Well, I'm
excited to have you both.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.382
Today, we will be
answering questions

00:01:12.382 --> 00:01:16.030
submitted by you, our amazing
Google Cloud YouTube audience.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.760
We're always looking out for
questions about Google Cloud

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.520
products, so be sure
to leave any questions

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.980
you may have in the
comments of our videos

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.770
using the hashtag,
AskGoogleCloud.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.140
And if you have any other
questions that come up

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.420
as you're watching today,
then please go ahead

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.310
and type them in the live chat.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.350
I think it's probably
somewhere over here.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.270
And we'll do our best to
try to get to all of them.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.080
We'll be hosting AskGoogleCloud
segments throughout the year,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.030
so be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.580
to get notified every time
a new episode goes out.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.030
So with that said,
let's go ahead and get

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.070
started with
today's topic, which

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.860
is AI and machine learning.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.080
Our first question comes
from Vedic_ who asks,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.180
how do you get started with
AI/ML with a non-technical

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:02.120
background?

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.810
So Dale, let's go ahead and
throw this question to you

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:05.780
first.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.780
DALE MARKOWITZ: I get
this question all the time

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.430
and my answer really
changes depending

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.370
on what you're going for,
what your background is.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.760
As a developer-- which is
a technical background--

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.170
I always like to
tell people, you

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.138
should go at machine
learning by trying

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:21.430
to find a project that you
actually wanted to do anyway

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:25.490
that uses ML, and then just use
the simplest tool for the job.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.790
So start with
high-level tools-- maybe

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.900
speech-to-text API, maybe a
pre-built image classification

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:32.403
model.

00:02:32.403 --> 00:02:34.570
And then once you get the
feel for how ML is useful,

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.600
you'll be more motivated to go
in and learn TensorFlow and do

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.700
the nitty-gritty details.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.527
But I'm sure, Sara, you
have thoughts on this.

00:02:42.527 --> 00:02:43.360
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.310
I think one of the cool
things that I've seen recently

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:47.560
is there's a lot of tools
that are just making machine

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.050
learning more accessible
to people that may not

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:51.550
have a technical background.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.290
So things like
AutoML, which we'll

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.290
get into probably later on--

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.468
really help a lot with that.

00:02:57.468 --> 00:02:59.260
DALE MARKOWITZ: There's
also a tool I adore

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.650
called teachable machines.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:01.900
In fact--

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.090
SARA ROBINSON: I've seen
that one, it's awesome.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.110
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.818
It's like a way for
you to train a model,

00:03:06.818 --> 00:03:09.630
like an audio or a vision
model, and it's like--

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.150
I had a 10-year-old
kid email me a model

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.270
that he trained using it.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:17.000
So it's-- he's very smart,
but it is a very user-friendly

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:17.500
tool.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.333
SARA ROBINSON: There's
also a great resource

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:21.850
called Machine Learning
Crash Course, which

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.742
is made available by Google.

00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:25.450
It used to be just
internal training that

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.180
was used at Google, but we
made it public a few years ago.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.310
I'm constantly going to
the glossary on that site

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.328
just to remind myself common
machine learning terms.

00:03:33.328 --> 00:03:34.870
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, that's awesome.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.910
I mean, if a
10-year-old can do it--

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.470
although he is
super smart-- then

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.840
I feel like that's motivation
for us to get started.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.540
And believe it or not, I was
always OK at math and stats

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.513
and I wasn't classically
trained in technical skills,

00:03:47.513 --> 00:03:48.430
I guess you could say.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.310
So, it's nice to know that
I can do machine learning

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.803
by using all of these resources
that you just listed online.

00:03:54.803 --> 00:03:57.220
I think now, more than ever,
there is a plethora of things

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.533
that you can find.

00:03:58.533 --> 00:03:59.950
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Well, Stephanie, I

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.260
don't think you should hold
other people to your bar,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.370
because it's pretty high.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.593
But I get what you're saying.

00:04:05.593 --> 00:04:06.760
STEPHANIE WONG: Well thanks.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.090
I mean, we all have to
start somewhere, right?

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:13.600
OK, let's go ahead and move
on to our next question, who

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.760
is from Crikxouba--

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.170
I think that's how
you say it-- who asks,

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.320
can you clarify what's the
difference between Vertex

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.470
AI and your old AI platform?

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.210
This is so confusing!

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.940
OK, I'm going to admit
that I was a bit confused

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.000
at first too, but it's actually
quite clear once explained.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.247
So Sara, we'll go
to you for this one.

00:04:33.247 --> 00:04:34.330
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, sure.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.340
So I've been getting
this question a lot

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.473
since Vertex AI was announced
a few weeks ago at Google I/O.

00:04:40.473 --> 00:04:41.890
So basically, how
you can think of

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.700
it is that Vertex AI includes
everything from the AI platform

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.000
that you know, but it
also includes AutoML.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.130
And the best part is
that it includes a lot

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.410
of new products for MLOps--

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.370
ML operations.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.250
So, MLOps is
basically the practice

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.620
of productionizing your
models in a way that's

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.540
scalable and reproducible.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.780
So the MLOps products
that are part of Vertex AI

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.090
include Pipelines,
Feature Store,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.400
model monitoring-- maybe
some others in there

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.780
that I'm forgetting.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.300
And then one of my favorite
parts about Vertex AI is that

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.850
whether you're training
a model-- it's AutoML,

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.550
or you wrote your own custom
model code in something like

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.380
TensorFlow or PyTorch--

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.580
all of those models show
up in the same unified UI,

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.890
and they can be deployed
to the same end points.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.200
So it's a much more
streamlined experience.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.390
We definitely
recommend that you use

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.520
Vertex AI for all
of your new machine

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.170
learning projects
on Google Cloud.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.470
You can also migrate your
old models from AutoML, or AI

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.085
Platform to Vertex AI.

00:05:40.085 --> 00:05:40.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.000
I mean, I've gotten
the question a lot too,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.830
and I like to tell
people that you

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.020
can think about it like an
evolution of AI Platform

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.610
Unified so that--
like you said--

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.010
you have one experience in
interface for both AutoML

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:54.340
and creating custom models, but
it's all shared infrastructure,

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.850
data modeling UX, and API
layer so that you can quickly

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.590
move from data
science to production,

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.600
and it's useful for ML
novices with AutoML.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:06.330
But then, as you become
more experienced,

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.080
you're able to manage
constraints and make

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.790
your own fine-grained
decisions with Vertex AI.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:11.290
So, yeah.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:12.220
This is exciting.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:12.850
SARA ROBINSON:
Yeah, you bring up

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.500
a good point about experience.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:19.690
So it's meant to enable you to
use ML, regardless of your ML

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:20.280
expertise.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.822
So if you're new to
machine learning,

00:06:21.822 --> 00:06:24.823
there's tools for you on
Vertex AI that you can use.

00:06:24.823 --> 00:06:26.740
If you have more ML
expertise and are building

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.240
your own custom
models from scratch,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.570
you can use Vertex AI as well.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.465
It works across the board.

00:06:32.465 --> 00:06:34.840
DALE MARKOWITZ: And just to
riff off what you said, Sara,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.120
I think we've been thinking
about how you build

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.520
and deploy machine learning
models for a long time.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.770
You need GPUs, you need
hardware, blah, blah, blah.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.040
But the idea behind Vertex
is, if you're actually

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.570
going to use an ML
production, there

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.120
are all sorts of other things
you probably would find useful

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.820
that are not necessarily
the ML part-- like, keeping

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:54.653
track of your data sets
and how they've changed,

00:06:54.653 --> 00:06:57.070
and what is your model doing
when it's making predictions,

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:00.308
keeping a log of how the
data distribution is,

00:07:00.308 --> 00:07:01.350
and whether it's drifted.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.447
The idea with Vertex is,
yeah, you get the model,

00:07:03.447 --> 00:07:05.530
but then we get all these
other useful things that

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:10.960
integrate really nicely
in one spot and one API.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.620
STEPHANIE WONG: Well, speaking
of integrations in one spot,

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.960
this is a perfect segue
Because our next question is

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.780
from Chiru B, who
asks, why would I

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:23.170
use Vertex Pipelines when
I can use Vertex Training

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:25.970
and Prediction--
all parts of Vertex.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.132
So Sara, why don't you actually
start by answering that first?

00:07:29.132 --> 00:07:31.090
SARA ROBINSON: First let
me start by describing

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.100
what Vertex Pipelines is.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.110
So Vertex Pipelines
essentially lets

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.750
you run end-to-end
machine learning workflows

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:40.720
on Google Cloud using
Google Cloud infrastructure.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.380
And you can write your pipelines
using popular Python libraries,

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.530
like Kubeflow Pipelines
or TensorFlow Extended.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:52.940
And so a pipeline consists
of steps, or components.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:56.470
So you break each ML task
in your pipeline down

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:59.170
into a series of these steps.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.900
And so, one step might be
for importing your data set,

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.790
one step might be
for training, then

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.100
one step for
deploying your model.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.230
So that's the purpose
of Vertex Pipelines.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:09.940
And what you can do
is you can actually

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:13.750
have a step that would use
Vertex Training and Vertex

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:14.360
Prediction.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.630
So say you have a model
that you're training.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.910
You want to have one
of your components

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.580
use the Vertex AI
training service to train

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.968
your model at scale.

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:24.760
And then maybe you want
to have a step that

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.740
evaluates your model and
looks at a threshold.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.560
So maybe you only want
to deploy your model

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.429
if accuracy is above, say, 95%.

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:35.500
So you can actually
add conditional logic

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:39.039
to your pipeline, and then
say check my model accuracy,

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:41.715
or you can define whatever
metric you'd like.

00:08:41.715 --> 00:08:43.090
See if it's above
this threshold.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:46.720
If so, deploy it to
Vertex Prediction.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.970
But you can also use Vertex
Training and Prediction

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:49.640
on their own.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.400
You don't have to
use it in a pipeline.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:54.910
So what I probably recommend
is, as you're getting started--

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.220
prototyping, developing
your machine learning

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.367
models and workflows--
start by just using Training

00:09:00.367 --> 00:09:01.450
and Prediction standalone.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:03.490
And then when you're
ready to scale that--

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.650
and you've devised
what all the steps you

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:07.480
want in your pipeline to be--

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.970
then convert that
into a pipeline

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.710
and run it on Vertex Pipelines.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.363
Hopefully that clears things up.

00:09:13.363 --> 00:09:14.530
DALE MARKOWITZ: I also add--

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:15.970
I'm so sorry, Stephanie.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.800
I also wanted to add
to what Sara said--

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.180
and tell me if you agree--

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.800
that I feel like with software
development, there's often--

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.560
especially developing
for the Cloud--

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.800
there's often this trade off
between how easy something is

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.460
to get working,
and how easy it is

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:33.050
to port between different
running environments.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.380
So if you were having a
machine learning pipeline

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.560
running on-premises
in Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.010
you can move it from your
local on-prem environment

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.730
to Google Cloud more easily.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.910
But it's probably more
work to set up pipelines

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.310
than it is to just use
Vertex's prediction service.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:50.050
Maybe you get that
running really fast,

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:52.540
but then maybe that's harder
to remove from Google Cloud.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:55.690
So, there's sort of this
compromise between portability

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:57.152
and ease of use.

00:09:57.152 --> 00:09:59.110
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, I
totally agree with that.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.802
If I was building
something on my own,

00:10:00.802 --> 00:10:03.010
maybe I would start by using
training and prediction.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.500
But then let's say I wanted
to share it with you, Dale,

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:08.260
and Stephanie so that you could
run it on your own machine.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.260
It's going to start to
get a little complicated.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:12.730
I need to make sure that you're
using similar environments

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.133
and have dependencies set up.

00:10:14.133 --> 00:10:15.550
So in that case,
I might just want

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:18.280
to create a pipeline that
each of you could kick off,

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.010
and we could track
all of the output

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.770
from that pipeline
across different runs.

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:26.590
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, I
love the option to have

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.000
that collaborative approach.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:29.680
And what I love about
Vertex Pipelines

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.660
is that it's giving you MLOps--

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.220
which we've touched
on and talked

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:36.250
about here-- aka applying
DevOp strategies to ML systems

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:38.530
so that you can build
and release code changes,

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.540
monitor systems and reliability.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.270
And like you said, it goes
hand-in-hand with training,

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:45.790
because you can reuse
a pipeline's workflow

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.140
to train a new model.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:49.390
So I love how it's
all integrated

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:50.920
into each of these parts.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.230
Let's go ahead and move on
to the next question, which

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:56.170
is from Aravind
S., who asks, how

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:59.890
do I use image data with
natural language processing?

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:02.078
And we'll go to
Dale for that one.

00:11:02.078 --> 00:11:03.620
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Interesting question.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.537
There are a lot of ways
to answer it depending

00:11:05.537 --> 00:11:06.550
on how you interpret it.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.490
So one would be, is
there text in the image?

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:11.350
Because that's a
matter of maybe using

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.360
OCR to extract
the text, and then

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.120
you just have a normal natural
language processing problem.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.100
But maybe you're
actually asking about,

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.980
what are some cool machine
learning techniques

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.500
to use images and text together?

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:25.570
I love this question,
because it's something

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:28.630
that lots of people are thinking
about in the ML space now.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.180
Normally, we kind of
weirdly siphon the way

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:33.340
we think of ML
problems into here's

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:35.860
the set of video problems,
and image problems,

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:38.140
and audio problems,
and text problems.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:40.540
But in reality, if you're
trying to take advantage

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.240
of data that's in the
world or on the web,

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.150
a lot of times what you have
is a multimodal data set.

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:50.270
You have images with captions
is one perfect example.

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:54.100
So, for example, learning
how to caption images

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:57.560
is one really neat combination
of these two modalities.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:00.730
One trend that I see that's
exciting today is the ability

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:03.820
to find ways to use machine
learning to represent data--

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:05.800
like images and text--

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.860
so that what you learn
from text transfers

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:10.390
to what you learn from images.

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:14.760
For example, if you have a model
that looks at texts and learns

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:21.000
that usually, men are the
ones that have mustaches--

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:22.520
this is a very
contrived example--

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:24.270
you learn something
about the relationship

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:25.482
between facial hair and men.

00:12:25.482 --> 00:12:26.940
Then maybe when
you see images, you

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.680
can transfer what you
read about in text

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.220
to the images and vise-versa.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.480
So, basically, humans learning
from audio text and images,

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.720
and maybe machine learning
models can now too.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:41.020
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, so the next
question is from Pascal L., who

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.540
asks, how can you use
data from Google Cloud

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:48.100
Storage or BigQuery to train
models on Google Cloud?

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:48.980
I love this question.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.785
So let's go ahead and
go to Sara for this one.

00:12:51.785 --> 00:12:53.160
SARA ROBINSON:
Thanks, Stephanie.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.440
So all of our services
across Google Cloud

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.180
are really well
integrated, which

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.030
makes it easy to import data
from other places in Google

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:03.050
Cloud when using it for
training models in Vertex AI.

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:05.670
So when you're creating a
new data set in Vertex AI,

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:09.160
you have a few options for where
you want to import your data.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.250
You could either import a file
locally from your computer,

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.720
you could provide a link to a
file in Google Cloud Storage,

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.490
or you could even provide the
URL of a table in BigQuery.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:19.990
So for those of you
that don't know,

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.100
BigQuery is our
enterprise data warehouse.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.590
So you can connect
your BigQuery data set

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.570
to your data set in Vertex AI.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.210
No need to download the
data and reupload it.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.308
Vertex AI will handle
that integration for you.

00:13:33.308 --> 00:13:35.100
And then one other
thing I want to call out

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.992
is we have a tool called
BigQuery Machine Learning.

00:13:37.992 --> 00:13:39.450
So what this lets
you do is it lets

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.060
you take your structured
data in BigQuery,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.550
and use that to train custom
machine learning models.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.258
And you don't have to
worry about writing

00:13:46.258 --> 00:13:47.880
that underlying model code.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.800
That's all handled for
you by BigQuery ML.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.440
You just choose the type
of model you want to use.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.800
And one other thing
I'll mention is that--

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.690
related to services
being well integrated--

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:02.070
if you're using a Vertex AI
notebook or a hosted JupyterLab

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:05.550
Notebook solution and
all of your resources

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:08.385
are in the same project as
that hosted notebook instance,

00:14:08.385 --> 00:14:10.260
you don't have to worry
about authenticating.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.340
That's all handled for
you within notebook.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.910
So let's say I want to take
my data set in BigQuery

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.180
and download that into a Pandas
DataFrame within my notebook.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.480
I can do that easily using
the hosted notebook solution.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.330
DALE MARKOWITZ: I think as a
lazy machine learning engineer,

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:26.130
I-- and surely
many other people--

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.890
underestimate how much
of a big difference

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:31.350
this makes when
your data is kind

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.480
of in the same place
as your notebooks,

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:36.090
and your notebooks are
hosted on the Cloud

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.295
and you don't have to be
constantly refreshing them.

00:14:38.295 --> 00:14:40.170
It's really-- really
makes you more efficient

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:41.785
when it's all in the same spot.

00:14:41.785 --> 00:14:42.660
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:44.993
There's something to be said
about a seamless experience

00:14:44.993 --> 00:14:48.570
without context switching and
the authentication built in.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:51.180
It's integrated-- I think--
between Google Cloud

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.010
Storage, BigQuery, and Vertex.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:55.920
You have options, whether
it's batch or streaming data,

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:57.630
pulling it from
Google Cloud Storage,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.790
or using the console
or API to upload

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.710
from your computer
or Cloud Storage

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:05.380
and then make modifications
and start the training process.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:09.060
So the next question is
from LG Good, who asks,

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:12.810
is it important to know
TensorFlow and PyTorch?

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:15.267
So, Sara, can you
answer this one?

00:15:15.267 --> 00:15:16.350
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, sure.

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:18.140
So the short
answer, I would say,

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:19.970
is that you don't
need know both.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.370
You can choose whichever
one is best for the job.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.370
There's definitely no harm in
learning both if you'd like to.

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:27.540
But both have really
active communities.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.272
So if you need help with
issues you're running into,

00:15:30.272 --> 00:15:32.480
there's a good chance you'll
be able to find support.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:36.050
And the good news is that
both TensorFlow and PyTorch

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.250
run on Google Cloud Vertex AI.

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:41.150
So if you write
models in either one,

00:15:41.150 --> 00:15:44.693
you can train and deploy
those on Vertex AI.

00:15:44.693 --> 00:15:45.860
STEPHANIE WONG: Easy enough.

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:49.920
So our next question is
from Chibi C., who asks,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.780
can I use Google Cloud to train
models and generate predictions

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.880
on text documents?

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.005
Dale, I have to go
to you for this one.

00:15:58.005 --> 00:15:58.880
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.505
Actually, we could
both talk about this

00:16:00.505 --> 00:16:03.590
because just the other week, we
both talked about document AI

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:05.420
on the GCP podcast.

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:09.950
And so, document AI is a tool
for everything machine learning

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:10.730
and documents.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:13.220
Whether something--
a paper document you

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:16.670
take a photo of
or a scanned PDF.

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:19.670
So it allows you to read
the text off of a page,

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:21.920
it allows you to
understand what text

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:23.693
means within the
context of a page,

00:16:23.693 --> 00:16:24.860
like maybe you have a table.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.235
Or, you have a
form, and you want

00:16:26.235 --> 00:16:28.130
to understand-- this
is the field birthdate

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:30.930
and this is the value
for within birthdate.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:31.430
So, yeah.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:32.472
The document AI toolset--

00:16:32.472 --> 00:16:34.930
I mean, I don't know, Stephanie,
what do you think of that?

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
so, once again, we just

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:39.110
talked about this
on the GCP podcast.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.210
And what fascinated
me about document

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.390
AI was that under the hood,
these pre-trained models that

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:47.720
are built by Google Cloud
are using computer vision,

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:51.320
including OCR and natural
language processing that

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.140
creates these high-value,
high-volume documents.

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.030
And then meanwhile,
on the podcast,

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.880
we also talked about
our knowledge graphs,

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:01.610
which validate and enhance
the fields in your documents,

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:04.520
and you can train and create
your own custom document models

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:08.000
and still have human interaction
with AI to ensure accuracy

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.380
when you need it.

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:10.260
Pretty cool stuff.

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:12.510
DALE MARKOWITZ: One of the
things that this reminds me

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:16.099
of-- now that you bring up the
knowledge graph-- is, I love--

00:17:16.099 --> 00:17:17.270
I'm a Kindle person.

00:17:17.270 --> 00:17:18.805
It's OK, I don't
need paper books.

00:17:18.805 --> 00:17:20.180
And one of the
things that I love

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.660
about reading a
book on a Kindle is

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.202
if I don't know a
word, I click on it

00:17:23.202 --> 00:17:24.618
and boom-- there's
the definition.

00:17:24.618 --> 00:17:26.210
It's like, maybe
connected to the web.

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:30.060
Maybe I even get a translation
right there in my book.

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:31.730
Document AI enables
a similar thing

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:34.190
where, maybe I'm
looking at an invoice

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:36.110
and there's a business
with an address.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.488
And I click something-- and
because of the integration

00:17:38.488 --> 00:17:41.030
between the data on the page
and the Google knowledge graph--

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.490
I know, is this an
active business,

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:45.020
and what type of
business this is

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:46.653
by linking it against web data.

00:17:46.653 --> 00:17:47.570
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:49.760
Right, it's not
just text anymore.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.670
It's this window into more
metadata and information

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:55.002
beyond that, which
we can represent

00:17:55.002 --> 00:17:56.210
through this knowledge graph.

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:57.680
So it's pretty amazing stuff.

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.150
Our next question comes
from Moe B., who asks,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:06.080
could you give tips on
operationalizing ML models?

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:08.690
How would you go about
it, and based on what?

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:09.740
So, Dale.

00:18:09.740 --> 00:18:11.885
How about we go to you?

00:18:11.885 --> 00:18:13.010
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, sure.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.052
I'm happy to answer this.

00:18:14.052 --> 00:18:16.393
I think your
biggest concern when

00:18:16.393 --> 00:18:18.560
you have a model in
production-- there are a couple.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:20.510
First of all, you
want to make sure

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:23.090
that the data that you're
making predictions against

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:25.610
is the same as the data
you trained your model on.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:28.382
This is one of the biggest
places that people have issues.

00:18:28.382 --> 00:18:30.340
They train their model
on some sample data set,

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:31.820
then when they get predictions
from the real world,

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:32.910
they're out of sync.

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:34.850
So when you're
operationalizing your model,

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:37.250
you want to, A, be able to
have a way to check for that.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:40.790
To be able to automatically,
over time as the world changes,

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:43.640
check for a drift
between your training

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:45.830
data and the real-world data.

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:48.590
And probably also a good
way to deal with the fact

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:50.360
that the world
changes is to have

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:52.697
a way of collecting the
new data as it comes in

00:18:52.697 --> 00:18:54.530
and you make predictions
against your model,

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:57.070
and then automatically
retraining your model

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.320
and updating it with new data.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:00.320
So that's the first thing
that comes to my mind.

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:02.840
Sara, I'm sure you have a
lot of thoughts on this.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:04.548
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
I would add to that.

00:19:04.548 --> 00:19:07.400
So everything you described,
you can do that in Vertex AI.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:10.730
You can automate this workflow
using Vertex Pipelines.

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:12.530
And one of the
cool MLOps services

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:14.300
that's available as
part of Vertex AI

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:16.243
is called model monitoring.

00:19:16.243 --> 00:19:17.660
So it lets you do
exactly what you

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:20.180
described, and detect drift.

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:24.107
So if the new predictions
that you're getting coming in

00:19:24.107 --> 00:19:26.690
are different from the data that
you use to train and evaluate

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:28.910
your model, chances
are you'll want

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:31.710
to find an automated way
to get alerted about that.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:33.500
So you can set up
alerts so that you'll

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:36.620
get notified when a
certain threshold is

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:40.940
reached to identify drift.

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:43.730
Then, you can kick
off model retraining,

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:45.645
do whatever you want
within a pipeline.

00:19:45.645 --> 00:19:46.520
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.100
And this all kind of
speaks to this other topic

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.790
that we recently
had on the podcast,

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:52.160
which was responsible AI.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:56.180
And to me, MLOps is this major
evolution in ML practices,

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.490
because you can not only
reduce the amount of time

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:02.180
it takes, but you can reliably
go from data ingestion

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:03.800
to deploying model
in production.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:07.797
And, like you said, monitor
and understand your ML system.

00:20:07.797 --> 00:20:09.380
It's reliable, it's
efficient, and you

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:12.020
get that explainability,
that transparency,

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:14.930
so that you can have these
healthier, more representative

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.332
practices and models.

00:20:17.332 --> 00:20:19.790
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, one thing
I actually forgot to mention

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.430
is one of the great
things on Vertex

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:24.133
AI is feature
attributions, which

00:20:24.133 --> 00:20:25.800
are available for
different model types.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.430
So for example, if you
train a tabular model

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:31.580
in Vertex AI using AutoML, you
can get feature attributions

00:20:31.580 --> 00:20:35.030
which basically show you which
features influenced your model

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:37.650
prediction the most and what's
signaling that prediction.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:39.537
So it really lets you
look under the hood

00:20:39.537 --> 00:20:41.120
and have a better
understanding of how

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:42.405
your models are performing.

00:20:42.405 --> 00:20:43.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:44.810
And you were able
to leverage some

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:47.930
of this for some of your past
projects, like the cooking one,

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:48.997
right?

00:20:48.997 --> 00:20:50.330
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:52.597
That was a fun one.

00:20:52.597 --> 00:20:54.680
DALE MARKOWITZ: This is
Sara's project originally,

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:58.460
but she invented new recipes
using machine learning,

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:02.100
and then we kind of
riffed on it together.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:03.740
You want to tell us about it?

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.097
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

00:21:05.097 --> 00:21:06.680
And you should all
check out the video

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:08.482
that Dale and I did on this.

00:21:08.482 --> 00:21:10.190
Basically, what I did
is I've been baking

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:13.100
a ton during the past year.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:15.710
And I started to notice that
baking recipes in particular

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:17.420
follow a lot of
distinct patterns.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:20.960
So there's a specific ratio
of flour to fat to sugar

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.218
that makes a cookie
distinct from a cake.

00:21:23.218 --> 00:21:25.760
So I wanted to see if I could
train a machine learning model.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.800
To start out, I trained
a model to predict

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.200
what different
recipes were, starting

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:31.580
with just three labels--

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.530
bread, cake, and cookie.

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:36.950
And then, I wanted to see
if I could use this model--

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:39.530
tweak the features,
the inputs, play around

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.520
to see if I could
invent a new recipe.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:44.733
So the first one I created
was this hybrid cake-cookie

00:21:44.733 --> 00:21:45.650
that I called a cakie.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:47.390
It was pretty delicious.

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:52.770
We attempted in the video,
and we also make a new one.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:53.700
It was fun.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.742
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, you
have to check that out.

00:21:55.742 --> 00:21:57.410
And I think you also
partnered with Mars

00:21:57.410 --> 00:21:59.810
and did some things
with confectionary

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.670
and you made some
recipes with that too,

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:04.040
so you've done a
lot in the space.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:06.158
SARA ROBINSON: Yes, big
fan of baking and ML.

00:22:06.158 --> 00:22:08.450
DALE MARKOWITZ: You may be
the premiere baking, machine

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:11.960
learning expert I
think in the world.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:13.558
SARA ROBINSON: It's
such a fun hybrid.

00:22:13.558 --> 00:22:15.350
Yeah, the Mars Project
was really cool too.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:18.350
Basically flipped the
model that Dale and I

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:19.670
described in the video.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.580
Flipped that.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.923
So instead of providing
ingredient amounts as input,

00:22:23.923 --> 00:22:25.340
you provide what
you want to make.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:29.210
So I would say, I want
a 50% cake, 50% cookie,

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:31.850
and it would tell you the amount
of certain base ingredients

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:33.200
that you need to make that.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.060
There's a blog post on it.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:36.380
We can provide a link.

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:40.272
STEPHANIE WONG: We might
need a spinoff show for you.

00:22:40.272 --> 00:22:41.480
SARA ROBINSON: I'm up for it.

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:43.438
STEPHANIE WONG: All right,
so our next question

00:22:43.438 --> 00:22:46.950
is from IDRL Indian
Drone Racing, who asks,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.730
how can we use less
images to train a model?

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:51.930
So, Dale, can you help
us out with this one?

00:22:51.930 --> 00:22:53.680
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah,
I think the question

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:57.340
is, how can we use less data
to train a model in general.

00:22:57.340 --> 00:22:59.345
And also, do we even
need to train a model?

00:22:59.345 --> 00:23:00.970
This is what I'm
always thinking about,

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.400
because if you follow
at all the projects

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:04.900
that I build using
machine learning,

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.240
I'm always trying to
avoid collecting data.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:09.050
This is the slowest
part of the project,

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:11.480
this is the most error-prone
part of the project,

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.320
it prevents you from
prototyping quickly.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:16.120
So I'm always wondering, how do
you get the most out of models

00:23:16.120 --> 00:23:17.260
without finding data?

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.780
So the answer that
everybody usually thinks of

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:21.760
is something called
transfer learning.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:23.510
To understand how
transfer learning works,

00:23:23.510 --> 00:23:26.320
you kind of have to understand
that the way that machine

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:28.180
learning works in
general is not that

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:30.460
just that you're learning
from data to understand

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:31.480
how to do a task--

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:34.522
like, identify
dog breeds, right?

00:23:34.522 --> 00:23:35.980
That a machine
learning model, when

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:38.020
it looks at tons of
images, not only is it

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:39.610
learning to identify
dog breeds, it's

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:43.720
also forming some understanding
of the world in general.

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:47.590
It's understanding that
animals have ears and two eyes,

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:49.070
and usually a nose in between.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:51.580
So in addition to trying
to learn what your task is,

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:53.960
it's also representing
the world.

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:56.770
This allows you to take
a model-- an image model,

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:57.372
for example--

00:23:57.372 --> 00:23:59.080
that was trained for
something completely

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:01.750
unrelated to
classifying dog breeds.

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.410
Maybe it's used to classify
all sorts of objects.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.290
And then, you bring your own
data with your own custom task

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:10.910
and you tweak and you tune
that model to do what you want.

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:12.910
So you take the model
that understands the world

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.682
and you say, OK, but now, find
a way to do my specific task.

00:24:16.682 --> 00:24:17.890
So this is transfer learning.

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:20.200
It's been used for a
couple of years now.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.930
Most image models will build
on top of another image model.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.883
But we're seeing this
even go farther now.

00:24:25.883 --> 00:24:28.300
Like, for example, there's
sort of a trending topic called

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:31.210
one-shot learning,
where the idea is

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:33.640
I want to get a model to
do my own custom task,

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.525
but I'm only going to
provide a couple of examples.

00:24:36.525 --> 00:24:38.650
I'm not going to provide
a whole training data set.

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:41.110
I'm going to depend on the
model being good enough

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:42.620
to be able to generalize.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:44.620
And if you look at
models like GPT-3,

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.920
this is a text model, a really,
really good multipurpose text

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:49.060
model.

00:24:49.060 --> 00:24:51.490
You can get this model
that was trained once

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:54.940
on one data set to write
code, write poetry, do

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:56.950
translation, be a chat bot.

00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:59.530
So we're moving forward
towards these trends in machine

00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:01.850
learning where can you
build a large model that's

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:04.360
really good-- that's so good,
in fact-- that you can use it

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:06.550
for multiple tasks without
finding training data?

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:09.030
That is my hope for
where we get to--

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:10.750
to answer the
question of how do you

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:13.805
avoid collecting
so much image data.

00:25:13.805 --> 00:25:14.930
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, yeah.

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:16.670
It's really exciting,
because I feel

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.628
like it's all about making
this more efficient,

00:25:18.628 --> 00:25:21.872
and the upfront work less on
the people who are doing it.

00:25:21.872 --> 00:25:23.330
And if I understand
correctly-- so,

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:26.540
transfer learning means
reusing a pre-trained model

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:28.740
on a new problem that
you're trying to solve.

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:31.940
So for image
classification or NLP,

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:34.580
this is a starting
point for you to help

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.515
develop a neural
network model on image

00:25:36.515 --> 00:25:37.640
or these types of problems.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:40.610
Because typically, there's
a lot of compute and time

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:42.875
resources required, right?

00:25:42.875 --> 00:25:43.750
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:45.460
You can kind of think
of it as, imagine

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:49.090
you need a data
scientist to write SQL,

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:51.610
but all you have is
a software engineer.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:54.310
But there's a lot of skills
that transfer between writing

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:55.707
SQL and writing Python code.

00:25:55.707 --> 00:25:57.790
So, it's better to start
with a software engineer.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:59.457
They're going to get
the basic concepts,

00:25:59.457 --> 00:26:04.003
and then you can train them with
less time to do the other task.

00:26:04.003 --> 00:26:05.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Right, well.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:06.730
There's a lot
happening in the space,

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:08.470
so I'm sure it's going to
get easier and easier as time

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:09.290
goes on.

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:12.730
So the next question
is from Taha, who asks,

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:15.400
is TensorFlow.js
the way to go if you

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:18.500
want to do real-time computer
vision on the browser?

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:21.100
I really wish I could
do that with OpenCV.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:22.675
So, Dale, let's start with you.

00:26:22.675 --> 00:26:23.800
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, sure.

00:26:23.800 --> 00:26:25.425
I guess you're asking
me because we've

00:26:25.425 --> 00:26:26.800
talked about a
recent project I'm

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:28.900
working on with TensorFlow.js.

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:32.440
What I love about TensorFlow.js
and also the tools built on top

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.060
of it, is that it's just--

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:36.250
machine learning part aside--

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.130
it just has a lot of nice
utilities for collecting

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:40.600
sensory data from the browser.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:43.750
Whether it's from the
camera, or for a microphone,

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:46.840
it has a library
structure to just hook

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:48.350
into that stream really quickly.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:52.660
And so it just makes any sort of
real-time sensory-based project

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:53.552
much easier to build.

00:26:53.552 --> 00:26:55.510
Have you have you guys
played with this at all?

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:57.290
SARA ROBINSON: I haven't
played with TensorFlow.js yet,

00:26:57.290 --> 00:26:58.810
but I have been meaning to.

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.850
I'm excited about your
new project on it.

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:03.160
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:04.513
DALE MARKOWITZ: Like a pet-cam.

00:27:04.513 --> 00:27:05.680
STEPHANIE WONG: I'm excited.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:08.055
They actually talked about
this in I/O pretty extensively

00:27:08.055 --> 00:27:09.490
when they were
covering AI, so it

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:12.670
seems like it's something
that is to be focused on.

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:15.190
The next question
is from Diego N.,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:19.150
who asks, how do I use AI
solutions in a small business

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:21.200
with a low budget?

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:23.367
So, Sara, can you
help with this one?

00:27:23.367 --> 00:27:24.700
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:26.295
This is a great question.

00:27:26.295 --> 00:27:27.670
So the first thing
I'll mention--

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:30.170
and this is available across a
lot of different Google Cloud

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:32.470
Services, not just
limited to AI and ML--

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.030
is to take advantage
of autoscaling.

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:37.153
So for example, when you're
deploying a model, when you're

00:27:37.153 --> 00:27:39.070
configuring the machine
type and the settings,

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:41.410
you can set that to auto
scale so that you'll only

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:44.140
be charged when you're
getting prediction

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:45.290
requests on your model.

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:47.530
And then if it's
not active, it'll

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:49.870
scale all the way
back down to zero.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.820
But I will also
mention that AutoML,

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:54.970
which we've talked about a
bit, is pretty cost-effective

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:57.670
and a great way to start
prototyping and developing

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:01.460
models that could eventually
become your production models.

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:05.050
So for example, if you want to
train an AutoML tables model,

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.780
an hour of training-- which
I find usually gets me pretty

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:09.130
good accuracy--

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:10.480
costs $20.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:17.050
And for video, text, and
vision, it's actually only $3

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:18.370
per hour of training.

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:19.660
Just found this out today.

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:22.420
Super excited about that.

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:23.570
Yeah, pretty awesome.

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:27.950
So another thing to think about
is your time is also valuable.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.640
So if you're going to be
spending time spinning up

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.460
a lot of this
infrastructure, it might

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:36.670
be a better use of your
time to just use the Cloud.

00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:39.250
Take advantage of managed
services to do that.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:41.890
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, I 100%
agree with what you just said,

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:42.940
Sara.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.850
Zooming back from the skews
on specific costs of products

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.100
and compute time or whatever,
the most expensive thing

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:49.683
that you could
possibly spend money on

00:28:49.683 --> 00:28:52.690
is probably buying a set of
PhDs or a data scientist, right?

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:53.648
SARA ROBINSON: Totally.

00:28:53.648 --> 00:28:56.080
DALE MARKOWITZ: So the way
you can really save money is--

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:57.140
especially when
you're starting off,

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:59.057
when you're building up
totally from scratch--

00:28:59.057 --> 00:29:02.590
use the pricier tool if
it's way easier to use.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:04.840
Build out the prototype, and
then bring in the experts

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:06.910
once the proof of concept works.

00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:07.630
SARA ROBINSON: Right,
because chances

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:09.547
are you want to focus
on your machine learning

00:29:09.547 --> 00:29:13.600
skills and improving and making
sure your data looks good.

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:15.340
And using managed
services really

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.470
let you focus on the
core of your machine

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:20.397
learning tasks, rather than
spinning up all that compute.

00:29:20.397 --> 00:29:21.980
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
I totally agree.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:25.120
I can't overstate the importance
of getting up and running

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.340
quickly, and then
utilizing managed services

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:30.010
and moving towards that
progression of hiring

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:30.940
the right people.

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:33.197
And, like we said
earlier, Vertex AI

00:29:33.197 --> 00:29:34.780
is that platform
that you can continue

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:38.620
to develop and progress as your
business gains more resources.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.440
So unfortunately, that's
